---
import Docs from "../../layouts/Docs.astro";
import Code from "../../components/Code.astro";
const download = "curl -fLO https://clinkcli.org/examples/ask_repo.py";
const fixture = [
  "git init demo-library",
  "printf '%s\\n' '# Demo library' 'Saved prompts are stored in SQLite.' > demo-library/README.md",
  "git -C demo-library add README.md",
  'git -C demo-library -c user.name=Demo -c user.email=demo@example.invalid -c commit.gpgsign=false commit -m "Add storage note"',
].join("\n");
const question = '"Where are saved prompts stored?"';
const args = "--repo demo-library --file README.md --first-line 1 --count 2";
const register = [
  "curl -fLO https://clinkcli.org/examples/repo_server.py",
  "uv venv --python 3.12 .venv",
  "uv pip install --python .venv/bin/python clink-cli==0.2.3",
  "claude mcp add --scope local --transport stdio repo-questions -- \\",
  '  "$PWD/.venv/bin/python" "$PWD/repo_server.py" \\',
  '  --repo "$PWD/demo-library" --file README.md --first-line 1 --count 2',
  "claude mcp get repo-questions",
].join("\n");
---

<Docs
  title="Build ask_repo"
  description="Give a script or an MCP client a repository question it can answer with checkable file references. Start with one committed file and one question."
  toc={[
    ["try", "Preview a packet"],
    ["ask", "Ask the agent"],
    ["verify", "Check the citations"],
    ["mcp", "Expose it as an MCP tool"],
    ["adapt", "Adapt the example"],
  ]}
>
  <p>
    <code>ask_repo</code> is a downloadable example built on{" "}
    <code>clink generate</code>. Its inputs are a local Git checkout, a tracked
    text file, a line range and a question. Each citation identifies the commit,
    file, line number and exact quote.
  </p>
  <h2 id="try">Preview a packet</h2>
  <p>
    You need Git and Python 3.10 or later to preview the evidence. Start in a
    new, empty working folder. Download the helper and create a two-line
    repository you can safely experiment with.
  </p>
  <Code label="Terminal · download the helper" code={download} />
  <Code label="Terminal · create the example repository" code={fixture} />
  <Code
    label="Terminal · local preview, no agent call"
    code={"python3 ask_repo.py --show-packet " + args + " " + question}
  />
  <p>
    The packet shows the commit and the two lines from <code>README.md</code>.
    Edit the file without committing and preview again. The packet still shows
    the original lines at <code>HEAD</code>.
  </p>
  <h2 id="ask">Ask the agent</h2>
  <p>
    Install Clink, then check your installed Codex executable against the{" "}
    <a href="/reference/#admissions">supported generation profile</a>. Sign in
    through Codex. This example requests <code>gpt-6.1-sol</code> explicitly.
    Approve only the demo folder and run the same question without the preview
    flag.
  </p>
  <Code
    label="Terminal · one native generation attempt"
    code={
      'uv tool install clink-cli\nclink generation-capabilities --json --cli codex --executable "$(command -v codex)"\nclink allow "$(cd demo-library && pwd -P)" --cli codex\npython3 ask_repo.py ' +
      args +
      " " +
      question
    }
  />
  <p>
    Use a trusted repository and prompt. Native generation runs in the selected
    checkout with the agent’s own configured tools and permissions. It can read
    more than the packet, change files or contact integrations. The instructions
    ask it to use the supplied evidence. Enforcing exclusive access to a packet
    requires separate isolation.
  </p>
  <h2 id="verify">Check the citations</h2>
  <p>
    A successful answer for the sample might say “Saved prompts are stored in
    SQLite.” Its citation must contain the actual commit and quote line 2
    exactly. The following is illustrative. Your checkout supplies its own
    commit ID.
  </p>
  <Code
    label="Example result · abbreviated for display"
    code={JSON.stringify(
      {
        answer: "Saved prompts are stored in SQLite.",
        citations: [
          {
            revision: "<commit from your checkout>",
            path: "README.md",
            line: 2,
            quote: "Saved prompts are stored in SQLite.",
          },
        ],
      },
      null,
      2,
    )}
  />
  <p>
    The helper checks completion, request identity, hashes and execution
    settings. It rejects invented line numbers, duplicate citations, mismatched
    quotations and truncated results. When the evidence is insufficient, the
    agent can return <code>{'{"answer":null,"citations":[]}'}</code>. A matching
    quote proves the cited text exists. Read it to judge whether it supports the
    answer.
  </p>
  <h2 id="mcp">Expose it as an MCP tool</h2>
  <p>
    Save{" "}
    <a href="/examples/ask_repo.py" download>
      ask_repo.py
    </a>{" "}
    and{" "}
    <a href="/examples/repo_server.py" download>
      repo_server.py
    </a>{" "}
    together. From the same working folder, create an environment with the
    pinned Clink release and register the example server. The registered command
    fixes the repository, file, range and Python environment. The calling agent
    supplies only the question.
  </p>
  <Code
    label="Terminal · register the companion with Claude Code"
    code={register}
  />
  <p>
    Open Claude Code in the directory where you registered the server. Ask it to
    call <code>ask_repo</code> on <code>repo-questions</code> with the arguments
    below. This server runs alongside Clink’s built-in MCP server, with its own
    registration and one tool.
  </p>
  <Code
    label="MCP arguments · custom ask_repo"
    code={JSON.stringify(
      { question: "Where are saved prompts stored?" },
      null,
      2,
    )}
  />
  <h2 id="adapt">Adapt the example</h2>
  <p>
    Point the helper at a different tracked UTF-8 file or adjust the line range.
    It accepts a file up to 64 KiB, at most 48 lines and a question up to 4,096
    bytes. It resolves one commit before reading any evidence. Paths, revision
    and quotes in the final citations come from that captured packet. A failed
    attempt returns an error to the caller and can have native side effects.
  </p>
  <p>
    The original repository pilot used an approved evidence manifest, separately
    configured requester and responder, and an additional response gate. This
    local example extracts its citation pattern. It makes one native call on
    your machine. You can also{" "}
    <a href="/extensions/">add a role or develop a built-in tool</a>.
  </p>
</Docs>
